    "comments": 4,
    "created_at": "2013-07-26T14:27:50Z",
    "updated_at": "2013-09-24T19:20:27Z",
    "closed_at": null,
    "body": "It's possible to trigger a situation where `can.view` converts the output of the internally called `can.view.render` to a document fragment twice, and tries to perform hookup of live binding twice.\r\n\r\nThis happens when you pass it a Deferred as the data *and* supply it a callback function. The code path taken will first resolve the deferred, which will pipe the view string through `can.view.frag` for hookup. Afterwards it will check if a callback was passed in and if so, will then also call it with the view string, which *again* will be piped through `can.view.frag` for hookup.\r\n\r\nOfcourse, this second time the `can.view.hookups` collection prepared by the view renderer will already have been consumed by the first fragment conversion and hookup. As a result, no hookup will take place the second time and the view will keep its mangled placeholders.\r\n\r\nI've mentioned this multiple times on multiple issues before, but I'll reiterate it again for this one: I strongly feel these kind of bugs could be completely avoided by sanitizing and unifying the `can.view` API to work exclusively through a Deferred and to **always** resolve results asynchronously."
