    "body": "Inspired from #364, I'd like to listen to changes of an observe within a compute and a deferred.  For example:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nvar personCompute = can.compute();\r\n\r\npersonCompute.bind(\"value.name\", function(ev, newVal, oldVal){\r\n  \r\n})\r\n\r\nvar person = new can.Observe({\r\n  name: \"Justin\"\r\n})\r\n\r\npersonCompute(person);\r\n\r\nperson.attr('name', \"David\");\r\n```\r\n\r\nI'd also like to be able to do this with a deferred:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nvar personDeferred = new can.Deferred();\r\n\r\npersonDeferred.bind(\"value.name\", function(ev, newVal, oldVal){\r\n  \r\n})\r\n\r\nvar person = new can.Observe({\r\n  name: \"Justin\"\r\n})\r\n\r\npersonDeferred.resolve(person);\r\n\r\nperson.attr('name', \"David\");\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn both cases, similar to can.Observe's current behavior, value.name events would not fire when changing the value of a compute or resolving the deferred. Instead, only when the value's name changed.  Although, it would be nice to change this as well someday.\r\n\r\nTo make this change, observe's bubbling of children would have to be reused in can.compute.  Deferreds would also have to be bindable.  `can.compute`'s change event would now be a \"value\" event and change would start resembling observe's change signature (ev, attr, how, newVal, oldVal). \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n"
